WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.559
Idea. On a cold day, it may be five degrees Celsius outside. On a hot day, thirty-five. But inside your body, it stays near thirty-seven. Homeostasis means keeping the internal environment constant, even when the outside changes. The internal environment is the conditions inside the body.

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:33.715
Constant does not mean it never moves. Body temperature goes up and down a little, but it is kept close to thirty-seven degrees. Blood glucose and the water in the blood are kept steady in the same way.

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:52.767
Method. How? By negative feedback. Start at the normal level. Then something changes it. A detector notices the change. A response happens that reverses the change. When the level is normal again, the response is switched off. This loop is negative feedback.

00:00:53.350 --> 00:01:13.601
Example one: body temperature. Normal is thirty-seven degrees. If the body is too hot, the hypothalamus detects it. The hypothalamus is a part of the brain. The response is sweating and wider arterioles in the skin. Arterioles are small arteries. If too cold, the response is shivering.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:33.159
Example two: blood glucose. If glucose is too high, the pancreas detects it. It releases a hormone called insulin. A hormone is a chemical carried in the blood. Insulin makes the liver store glucose as glycogen, a stored form of glucose, so glucose falls back to normal.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:50.526
Why keep the body at thirty-seven degrees? Because enzymes work best there. Enzymes are proteins that speed up reactions in cells. Too cold, and enzymes work slowly. Too hot, and enzymes are denatured: their shape changes and they stop working.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:02:03.829
Worked example. Now a real exam question. The question says: using the example shown in the diagram, explain what is meant by negative feedback. It is worth four marks.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:14.678
Here is the diagram. It shows how blood glucose is controlled. If glucose falls, hormone Y is released. If it rises, hormone Z is released.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:25.253
Pick one direction: blood glucose falls below normal. Less insulin is released. The pancreas releases glucagon, hormone Y.

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:33.859
Glucagon makes the liver turn glycogen back into glucose. So blood glucose rises until it reaches the normal level.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:42.661
Last point. Once the level is normal, the response is switched off. Glucagon release stops, and insulin release starts again.

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:49.393
Here is the full answer. Explanation by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:03:04.471
Common mistake. A common mistake is to say homeostasis means keeping the body the same. Say which part: the internal environment. And say it stays constant even when outside conditions change.

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:12.271
Another mistake is to say enzymes die when the body is too hot. Enzymes are not alive. They are denatured.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:25.537
Your turn. Ali swims in a very cold lake. His body temperature starts to fall. Using this example, explain what is meant by negative feedback. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:39.103
Answer. Ali's temperature falls below normal. His hypothalamus detects the change. In response, he shivers, and the arterioles in his skin get narrower, so less heat is lost.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:50.577
Shivering releases heat, so his temperature rises. When it reaches thirty-seven degrees again, the response stops. Cold water made it fall, and the body brought it back.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:04:00.951
So every time: a change from the normal level, a detector, a response that reverses the change, and the response stops once the level is normal.
